  "member_id": "svenningsson-per",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B005",
        "laureates": [
          "Kazutoshi Mori",
          "Peter Walter"
        ],
        "citation": "for discoveries of the unfolded protein response and signaling from the endoplasmic reticulum"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B052",
    "B013"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B052 to B005. The supplied discussion makes the unfolded protein response the stronger final configuration because it is a mature, fundamental, and broadly consequential mechanism with a more sharply defined discovery boundary and a compact attribution to Mori and Walter. Its importance for cellular adaptation to endoplasmic-reticulum protein-folding stress is durable across physiology and disease. B052 retains extraordinary medical importance, but the final comparison favors the achievement whose scientific core and credited contributors can more coherently be specified.",
    "case_against": "UPR research encompasses many important investigators and later pathway-defining contributions. The proposed citation must therefore remain restricted to foundational UPR signaling rather than the entire proteostasis field, and the adequacy of crediting Mori and Walter alone remains the central historical uncertainty. B005 also lacks the discrete, immediately visible therapeutic transformation represented by B052 and B013.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson ranked B005 first, arguing that it is a fundamental, mature mechanism with a coherent two-person attribution, while identifying the broader UPR field as the principal attribution reservation.",
        "response": "I agree with this distinction. On reassessment, the relative clarity of the proposed mechanistic achievement outweighs my earlier preference for the greater direct clinical scale of B052. The reservation is not removed: further historical delimitation is needed to ensure that the citation recognizes the foundational signaling discoveries rather than absorbing later work."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "sandberg-rickard",
        "point": "Sandberg favored B005 because its conserved signaling mechanism is mature and broadly relevant, while cautioning that the field's breadth makes a two-person award selective.",
        "response": "This is persuasive because it applies the same attribution discipline to B005 that the discussion has applied to the translational candidates. The relevant question is not whether UPR research had many contributors, but whether Mori and Walter can represent the defined foundational discovery; on the supplied record, that configuration is presently more defensible than the broader GLP-1 development chain."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius favored B013 for its long-established therapeutic transformation and relatively focused connection between a pathogenic hypothesis and clinical demonstration, while recognizing wider contributors to TNF biology and therapy development.",
        "response": "B013 remains an important alternative because durable clinical benefit deserves substantial weight. I nonetheless place B005 ahead: both candidates require historical boundary-setting, but B005 is framed as a foundational biological signaling mechanism with a more integrated two-person attribution, whereas the TNF therapeutic-development pathway retains a broader network beyond the proposed pair."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review would confirm that Mori and Walter alone sufficiently delimit the foundational UPR signaling discoveries.",
      "Whether the exceptional medical scale of B052 should outweigh its less sharply bounded attribution after separating foundational physiology from medicinal chemistry and broader clinical development.",
      "Whether B013 can be delimited from the wider history of TNF biology and biologic-drug development with comparable precision."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict with a proposed laureate is apparent from the supplied profile. The profile documents research connections with committee member Thomas Perlmann, not with a candidate; this does not alter my assessment on the supplied evidence."
  }
}
